 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Today was a pretty hairy day for me today.  Here's what happened:<br>
<br>
I was supposed to pick up my parents today at 3:00 at the airport, which is about 1.5 hours away in St. Louis.  I wanted to take my son with me, but my wife didn't want me to take him.  We argued.  She won.<br>
<br>
I'm probably about 20 minutes from the airport, just about to cross from Illinois to Missouri, when there's a bunch of traffic due to construction.  I'm sitting there, moving a few inches at a time, jamming to so Moby on my stereo, when I  glance in my rearview mirror and see something that would scare the djoy out of any driver.  A big-rig that's way too close to me, and moving too fast to stop in time to keep from hitting me. He wasn't like barreling towards me, but when you see something that big about to hit you, it doesn't matter how fast he's going.  It's scary as hell.<br>
<br>
The hit wasn't too bad.  I barely avoided hitting the guy in front of me, but it tossed my tapes and stuf around my car.  I wasn't hurt, but I was sure as hell shook up.  After the guy in front of me moved, I pulled over to the side.  Which is when the really givled up part started.  The guy started driving past me.  First, I though "Ok, he's going to pull in front of me.  There's more room."  But he kept going.  So I pulled in behind him and followed through the traffic.  I was thinking "Ok, there wasn't much shoulder there, he'll pull off at the next exit."  So, a few minutes later when we passed the rest-stop exit and traffic had started moving, I thought, "WTF?!"  So, I pulled alongside the guy and started honking.  I kept honking util I got his attention, and flagged him over.  He made motions like he didn't know what I was talking about, and then he finally looked like he was saying "Ok, I'll pull over."<br>
<br>
So, I got back behind him and waited.  I drove behind him past 3 good exits, none of which he pulled off at.  So, I pulled next to him again, and told him to pull over again.  He held up a phone and it looked pretty much like he told me "My boss says not to pull over."<br>
<br>
That's when I pulled out my cell phone and called 911.  I first got the local police.  I told them what happened, and they transferred me to St. Louis police (we had now gone all the way to north St. Louis and were about to leave it).  They were going to transfer me to the next city, but then transferred me to highway patrol.  Who then transferred me to St. Charles PD (which was the city that we'd just entered).<br>
<br>
St. Charles PD finally found an officer close enough to intercept, and within a few minutes (after the 30 that I'd spent following him up to this point), they finally had the guy pulled over.  They talked with him for a while, while I had to sit and wait in my car.  They finally came and got me, and told me the guy said he didn't hit me.  So we went to look at the damage.  Luckily, there wasn't really any visible damage (I now think my trunk may be slightly screwed, though).  However there WAS a definite impression of his license plate on my back bumper.<br>
<br>
The guy finally said he was sorry and that he didn't know he hit me (which I buy...you have no feedback in those things) and that he was going to the next truck scales (which I don't buy, because he didn't make any motion for me to follow him the last time I tried to flag him over).  <br>
<br>
Unfortunately, the Missouri police couldn't take an accident report, because the accident occured in Illinois.  The Illinois police were willing to send someone over, but there wasn't much they could do because I'd suffered no damage.  I ended up just getting the guy's insurance info and the name of one of the officers who'd testify that the guy definitely did hit me.  I have to call the guy's company on Monday to see what they'll do for me.<br>
<br>
I'm doing fine now, and the car is OK, but I was definitely glad I didn't have my son with me (chalk one up for mom's intuition). <br>
<br>
